---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="How I did this — Petty Evals">
  <div class="mb-12">
    <span class="sticker sticker--blue">methodology</span>
    <h1 class="display text-5xl md:text-7xl mt-3 mb-4">
      How I did this<br>
      <span style="color: var(--color-coral);">(the actual nerd shit)</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg max-w-2xl" style="color: var(--color-muted);">
      I tried to make a real eval, but for stupid questions. The methodology is genuine —
      Wilson confidence intervals, Cohen's kappa, the works. The questions are not.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 class="display text-3xl">The setup</h2>
    <p>For every fight I run the same recipe.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>4 models</strong>: Claude Sonnet 4.6, GPT-5.4, GPT-5.4 mini, Gemini 2.5 Pro.
      <strong>3 prompt variants per fight</strong> — different coding tasks that should provoke
      the same formatting choice (e.g., "write a function that reverses a linked list"
      and "write a class that implements a stack" both reveal indentation preference).
      <strong>2 temperatures</strong>: 0.0 (deterministic) and 0.7 (default).
      <strong>10 runs per cell.</strong> That's <strong>240 generations per fight</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      No system prompt, no priming. The model gets the question cold, like it would from a user.
      Then I measure what it actually wrote. Revealed preference, not stated preference —
      stated preference is sycophantic and boring.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">The classifier</h2>
    <p>
      Each output gets parsed: does the first indented line start with a tab, or with two-or-more
      spaces? That's the regex layer. When the regex says "I don't know" (e.g., the model produced
      wrapped prose without code), an LLM judge (Claude Haiku 4.5) breaks the tie.
    </p>
    <p>
      For every fight I hand-label a 30-sample subsample and compare to the classifier.
      <strong>Cohen's kappa &gt; 0.7 required</strong> for a fight to ship with a verdict;
      anything lower gets flagged as "classifier unreliable" and stays parked.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">The stats</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Vote per model</strong> = the modal classification across its 60 runs.<br>
      <strong>Confidence</strong> = % of runs that agreed with the modal vote.<br>
      <strong>95% CI</strong> = Wilson score interval.<br>
      <strong>Verdict</strong> = majority across models. Ties reported as ties — no tiebreaker.<br>
      <strong>Disagreement Index</strong> = normalized Shannon entropy of the votes.
      0 means unanimous; 1 means maximum chaos. The leaderboard sorts by this.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">The pundit panel</h2>
    <p>This part is the fun part.</p>
    <p>
      After all votes are in, I send each model a follow-up:
      <em>"Here's the result of the eval. One sentence on what you think."</em>
      Each model gets the full vote tally + one representative code sample per peer.
    </p>
    <p>
      I never edit the quotes. I may truncate at a sentence boundary. What you get is each
      model's character — Claude is polite, GPT is institutional, Gemini is enthusiastic,
      GPT-5.4 mini just wants to get back to work.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">Caveats</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>GPT-5.4 and GPT-5.4 mini are reasoning models.</strong> Temperature is silently
      ignored. Both calls are reported at the listed temperature for bookkeeping, but in practice
      the model picks its own sampling. I report this and you can decide whether to care.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is four models, not all models.</strong> Grok, DeepSeek, Qwen, and Opus are
      next in the queue once I get OpenRouter set up. (Then v1.1 is going to be a moment.)
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is the AI, not "developers".</strong> The AI is trained on a lot of human code,
      but it isn't a vote among humans. It's a vote among AIs about which humans they imitated.
      Make of that what you will.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">All the code</h2>
    <p>
      The whole pipeline is on <a href="https://github.com/kmelve/petty-evals">GitHub</a>.
      One command runs the whole thing: <code>pnpm evals --fight=&lt;slug&gt;</code>.
      Bring your own API keys, run it yourself, audit the numbers. That's the whole point.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
